answer:
**Requirements.** A board of 100 squares, snakes (head to lower tail) and ladders (bottom to higher top), N players taking turns with a dice, first to reach exactly 100 wins. Clarify: exact roll needed to finish or overshoot allowed, extra turn on a six, and one or two dice.

**Entities**
- `Dice`: `roll()` returns 1 to 6 (inject a random source so tests are deterministic).
- `Board`: size and `Map<Integer,Integer> jumps` holding both snakes and ladders, with `next(position)` returning the final square after any jump.
- `Player`: name and position.
- `Game`: a queue of players, board, dice; method `play()` returns the winner.

Validate the board on creation: no jump starts at 100 or at a square that another jump ends on, and no cycles.

```java
class Game {
    private final Board board; private final Dice dice;
    private final Deque<Player> turn = new ArrayDeque<>();
    Game(Board b, Dice d, List<Player> ps) { board = b; dice = d; turn.addAll(ps); }

    Player play() {
        while (true) {
            Player p = turn.pollFirst();
            int target = p.position + dice.roll();
            if (target <= board.size) p.position = board.next(target);
            if (p.position == board.size) return p;
            turn.addLast(p);
        }
    }
}
```

The queue gives fair turn order and supports any number of players, three in the example. A map of jumps makes each move O(1). Extensions: strategy interfaces for dice and win rules, an observer for logging moves, a game-state enum, and dynamic board configuration.

**Merge sort.** Split the array in half recursively, sort each half, then **merge** two sorted halves with two pointers into a temporary array. Time is O(n log n) in all cases, extra space O(n), and it is stable. For small arrays, the number of merge levels is about log2(n), so 8 elements need 3 levels. For the array above, an O(n log n) algorithm is fine; for tiny inputs insertion sort is also reasonable.
